.TH LOGON 1
.SH NAME
logon \- log on to Inferno
.SH SYNOPSIS
[
.B wm/wm
]
.B wm/logon
[
.B -l
] [
.BI "-n nsfile"
] [
.BI "-u user"
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Logon
logs a user in to the Inferno environment.
It requires
.I wm (1)
to be started first.
If no
.I user
name is specified by the
.B -u
option,
.I logon
displays a login panel to prompt for one.
The user name must have a directory
.BI /usr/ user,
which will become the current directory.
(Otherwise,
.I logon
will display a diagnostic panel and prompt again.)
The user name is written to
.B /dev/user
(see
.IR cons (3)),
which is the name presented on subsequent attaches to file servers.
.PP
Normally,
.I logon
expects keyboard input to provide a name,
but if the
.B -l
option is given,
.I logon
displays a list of the names in
.BR /usr ,
allowing one to be selected using a mouse or touch screen.
.PP
Once the current directory has been set,
.I logon
creates a new name space for the user using the contents of
.I nsfile
(default:
.BR namespace ),
as described in
.IR namespace (6).
It then starts
.IR toolbar (1)
to provide the initial application environment.
.SH FILES
.TF /dev/userxx
.TP
.B /dev/user
Inferno user name
.TP
.BI /usr/ user
.IR user 's
home directory
.SH INFERNODE
InferNode's
.I wm/logon
is a different program from the original described above: a secstore
unlock screen that establishes the user's encrypted key store under
.B /usr/inferno/secstore
(see
.BR /doc/AUTHENTICATION.md ).
It does not require or create
.BI /usr/ user
itself; user homes are durable via the
.B ~/.infernode
overlay and are created with
.IR newuser (8).
The original behaviour documented above still describes upstream
Inferno and native ports.
.PP
An unlock reads the account's key file from the secstore, loads its keys into
.IR factotum (4)
and tells factotum to save there.
An account with no key file yet unlocks with nothing to load.
A key file that is there and cannot be read (the server's error, a connection
that ended part way, a file of no bytes) is a failed unlock, with the reason
shown: the password may be tried again or the screen skipped, and nothing is
saved over a file that was not read.
A password with a blank in it is given to factotum quoted (the
.B secstoreq
request); a factotum that does not take it leaves saving off for the session,
and the screen says so.
What says whether a boot's keys were unlocked from the secstore, and where a key added now is saved, is
.B /mnt/factotum/secstore
(see
.IR factotum (4)).
No file is left in
.B /tmp
to mark an unlock: one was, which anybody could make and nothing reads any more.
.PP
A new account's password must be at least 8 characters.
An account with a second factor (see
.IR secstored (8))
is unlocked by its key or its recovery passphrase;
a key file under the password alone is taken for such an account only while its
second factor is being enrolled or disabled, not once it has been read under the factor's data key.
A key file in one of the two formats before the current one is not read:
.B "auth/secstore m factotum"
moves it (see
.IR secstore (1)).
.PP
On a screen with no keyboard (a phone, the Raspberry Pi's touch panel)
the boot starts the keyboard on the screen,
.IR wm-osk (1),
beside it.
.I Wm/logon
reads
.BR /mnt/osk/status
and, while the keyboard shows, lays its form out in the screen above
the keyboard's strip, leaving out the logo and the lines along the
bottom when the room left is too little for them; when it goes the form
has the screen again.
.PP
It also offers the interfaces the boot can start:
.IP
.B wm/logon
[
.BI -i " file"
] [
.I interface ...
]
.PP
Given two or more
.I interface
names it shows them under the password field, the one named in
.I file
chosen (the first if it names none of them); a click, or the up and
down arrow keys, choose another.
When the screen ends with a login or a skip, the name chosen is
written to
.IR file ,
one line of text.
.I Wm/logon
starts nothing itself: the boot script,
.BR /lib/lucifer/boot.sh ,
names the interfaces
.RB ( Lucia ,
the agent's three zones, and
.BR Desktop ,
.IR wm (1)
with a shell) and starts the one the file names.
It keeps the file in
.BR /usr/\fIuser\fP/lib/interface ,
or
.B /usr/inferno/lib/interface
for a user with no home of their own.
A boot with
.B skiplogon=1
starts Lucia whatever was chosen, and one with
.BI interface= name
starts that interface; a phone or tablet offers Lucia alone.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/wm/logon.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR toolbar (1),
.IR wm-osk (1),
.IR wm (1),
.IR namespace (6)
